## What I Learned

This lab reinforced several foundational Linux concepts:

- Commands entered at the prompt are input to the shell.
- Commands can return successful output or error messages.
- `echo` can generate string output.
- Quotation marks can control how text is interpreted by the shell.
- `expr` can perform integer arithmetic.
- Spaces matter when constructing `expr` expressions.
- Bash interprets certain special characters before passing arguments to another command.
- Parentheses and wildcard characters may need to be escaped.
- Error messages can provide useful clues about which layer of a command failed.
- Experimenting beyond prescribed lab instructions helps reveal how the shell actually behaves.

The most valuable part of the exercise was seeing that a failed command can still provide useful information. The syntax error in my additional calculation helped me understand how Bash parses commands before another utility processes them.

